{% doc %}
  One ratio keyword, resolved to a CSS `aspect-ratio` value.

  ## Why this is not ten strings in each caller

  The names are the theme's vocabulary — every image control in every block and
  section offers the same list — and the numbers behind them live here, once.
  They used to live in two `case` blocks inside `snippets/image.liquid`, which
  was fine while an `<img>` was the only thing that had a shape.
  `blocks/image-card.liquid` gives the *card* the ratio, because its picture is
  a background rather than an element, and a third copy of "portrait means
  4 / 5" is the copy that ends up disagreeing with the other two.

      portrait       4 / 5
      3-4            3 / 4
      2-3            2 / 3
      9-16           9 / 16
      1-1            1 / 1
      5-4            5 / 4
      4-3            4 / 3
      3-2            3 / 2
      16-9           16 / 9
      aspect-ratio   the image's own ratio

  Anything else is passed through, so a caller with a genuine one-off can hand
  over `5 / 7` and it lands in the declaration untouched.

  ## The names this theme shipped with

  `square`, `landscape`, `wide`, `tall`, `long` and `adapt` were the old
  vocabulary. Every schema, template and caller in the theme has been moved off
  them, but they still resolve, and deleting them is not a tidy-up worth doing:
  an unrecognised keyword does not fail loudly here — it falls through `else`
  and is emitted raw, and `aspect-ratio: long` is a declaration the browser
  drops on the floor. The image then has no reserved box and the layout moves
  as it loads. Six `when` branches are cheaper than that.

  One of them changed meaning and is the reason to read this twice: **portrait
  was 3 / 4 and is now 4 / 5**. It is the only name in either list that means
  something different than it used to, so a stored `portrait` that predates the
  migration renders slightly shorter than it did. `3-4` is the name that keeps
  the old shape.

  @param {string} [ratio] - One of the names above, or a raw `aspect-ratio` value
  @param {image} [image] - Read for its own ratio when `ratio` is `aspect-ratio`
  @param {string} [fallback] - Used for `aspect-ratio` when the image has no ratio. Default: 1

  @example
    {%- liquid
      capture aspect
        render 'aspect-ratio', ratio: s.ratio, image: s.image
      endcapture
    -%}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  comment
    Two lists in one `case`, in this order: the ten names every image control
    offers, tallest first — the same order the merchant reads them in — and then
    the old vocabulary, kept resolving so that nothing already saved can reach
    the `else` branch and be emitted as an invalid declaration.

    Both sit outside the `case` rather than beside the branches they describe.
    A `case` takes `when` and `else`; anything else between them is parsed
    and thrown away, and a comment that depends on that is a comment betting the
    whole page on an implementation detail.
  endcomment
  assign key = ratio | default: 'aspect-ratio'

  case key
    when '9-16'
      assign value = '9 / 16'
    when '2-3'
      assign value = '2 / 3'
    when '3-4'
      assign value = '3 / 4'
    when 'portrait'
      assign value = '4 / 5'
    when '1-1'
      assign value = '1 / 1'
    when '5-4'
      assign value = '5 / 4'
    when '4-3'
      assign value = '4 / 3'
    when '3-2'
      assign value = '3 / 2'
    when '16-9'
      assign value = '16 / 9'
    when 'aspect-ratio'
      assign value = image.aspect_ratio | default: fallback | default: 1

    when 'adapt'
      assign value = image.aspect_ratio | default: fallback | default: 1
    when 'square'
      assign value = '1 / 1'
    when 'landscape'
      assign value = '4 / 3'
    when 'wide'
      assign value = '16 / 9'
    when 'tall'
      assign value = '2 / 3'
    when 'long'
      assign value = '9 / 16'
    else
      assign value = key
  endcase
-%}
{{- value -}}
